---
// Why prkit exists, in a few short paragraphs. Every claim here is one the sections below make
// in full, and the links jump to them.
---

<section class="manifesto section column" aria-label="Why prkit">
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Agents write most of the code now. One person can run twenty of them at once, and the pull
      requests land faster than anyone can read them. Reviews turn into a quick look and a green
      check, and bit by bit you stop knowing what is in your own codebase. You fall out of the loop.
    </p>
    <p>
      prkit keeps you in it. The pull requests that need you are spread over a work account, a
      client's account and your own, so prkit finds every account the GitHub CLI is signed in to and
      puts them in <a class="jump" href="#what">one inbox</a>, each one read through <strong
        >the account that found it</strong
      >.
    </p>
    <p class="pull">Your agent reads first. You still decide.</p>
    <p>
      Open a pull request and click <strong>Generate guide</strong>. The <a
        class="jump"
        href="#faq">Claude Code or Codex</a
      > you already use reads a copy of it on your Mac and walks you through it: an overview, a before
      and after flow, then chapters that take the diff one idea at a time, core change first. You
      understand the change instead of skimming it. When new commits land, the guide catches up,
      until the pull request is merged or closed.
    </p>
    <p>
      When reviewers leave comments, the same agent can fix them on its own checkout of the branch.
      prkit makes the commit, and <a class="jump" href="#local">nothing reaches GitHub</a> until you
      say so.
    </p>
    <p>
      Agents do the reading and the typing. The decisions stay yours. All of it runs on your Mac,
      on <strong>the plan you already pay for</strong>. There is no prkit account, no prkit server
      and no API key.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .pull {
    color: var(--primary);
    font-size: 1.1875rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.5;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }
</style>
